// launcher.proto — launcher.v1: what a daemon says to the launcher
// (Zettel.app, launcher/), the resident macOS app that posts every desktop
// banner under its own name and icon.
//
// THE LAUNCHER IS THE SERVER, AND IT OUTLIVES EVERY DAEMON. It accepts on
// one unix socket that a socket holder owns for good
// (app.zettel.sockethold.launcher; launcher/requirements.json), and every
// daemon dials that socket to post a banner. So a daemon standing down, or
// both daemons of a handover, post exactly like a daemon that is serving:
// none of them needs a connection the launcher opened to it, and none can
// find the launcher gone because it was talking to another daemon. While the
// launcher restarts, a dial queues in the socket's backlog until it accepts.
//
// THE FRAMING. One exchange per connection, each message written as
// protobuf's standard length-delimited form (a varint byte count, then the
// message; Go's protodelim, Swift's BinaryDelimited): the daemon writes one
// PostBannerRequest, the launcher writes one PostBannerResponse and closes.
// The launcher answers only once macOS's notification center accepted or
// refused the banner, so the daemon's read of the answer is the post.

